  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThis Evening/Tonight...\n\nWinds are breezy this afternoon gusting 25-35mph ahead of an \napproaching shortwave trough. Temperatures are sitting in the 80s to \nlow 90s with dewpoints in the mid-upper 60s. A cold front/dryline is \nexpected to enter far northwestern portions of the area by the late \nafternoon hours. 12z Model guidance overall has trended away from \nsupporting isolated thunderstorm development this evening along the \naforementioned front. Given overall weak forcing and atmospheric \ncapping, if a storm does form in the area, it will likely be \nisolated. CAPE values of 2000 J/Kg combined with 25-30kts of shear \nwould support a severe threat (60mph wind gusts, quarter sized hail) \nwith any storm that does form. Moving into the overnight hours, \ncan't completely rule out a storm developing along the front, or \nstorms that develop north of the area (where forcing is stronger) \nsneaking into northeastern portions of the area. Given overall low \nconfidence in storms, kept PoPs limited to 15-20%. \n\nSaturday...\n\nSaturday morning, isolated-scattered showers with an occasional \nrumble of thunder are possible behind the frontal passage. These \nshowers will gradually shift southeast throughout the morning hours, \nexiting by the early afternoon. Breezy northerly winds are expected \nbehind the front, gusting 20-25mph. The cold frontal passage also \nresults in pleasant temperatures on Saturday, with highs in the 70s \n(NE) to low 80s (KS). As the Low Level Jet develops Saturday night, \nit brings additional thunderstorm chances to the area. A reservoir \nof elevated instability will be in place north of a frontal boundary \nin central/southern Kansas. It's plausible that a few storms \nSaturday night could tap into this instability and shear and \nbecome strong-severe though confidence on coverage of storms is\nuncertain. Storms chances (20-50%) Saturday night-Sunday \nmorning favor southeast/eastern portions of the area. \n\nSunday... \n\nLingering showers/storms will likely be ongoing across \neast/northeastern portions of the area to start the day on Sunday. \nRain will come to an end by the late morning hours. The surface \nfront in Kansas will begin to lift north during the day, reaching \nnorthern Kansas during the afternoon hours. Highs on Sunday will be \nin the 70s across Nebraska, with temperatures in the low-upper 80s \nin Kansas depending on the location of the frontal boundary. \nThunderstorm chances (20-50%) return Sunday night as the LLJ \nredevelops. \n\n\nNext Week...\n\nA more robust shortwave trough pushes into the Plains on Monday, \npushing a cold front into the area. Scattered showers/storms are \nfavored to develop along this front during the late afternoon-\novernight hours. The front pushes through the area Monday night, \nbringing more widespread rain chances. Confidence in the finer \ndetails is lower at this time but some strong-severe storms are \npossible. Cooler weather is expected Tuesday onwards behind the \nfrontal passage, with temperatures in the 60s and 70s. Off and on \nthunderstorm chances continue into the middle of next week as weak \ndisturbances traverse through the southwesterly-zonal flow.",
